{"lines": [{"text": "From the start?", "t": 5.703, "end": 6.663}, {"text": "All right.", "t": 6.983, "end": 7.783}, {"text": "A year ago I had forty windows open and couldn't find anything.", "t": 8.183, "end": 11.263}, {"text": "Then I installed a small program called Spaceman.", "t": 11.623, "end": 14.483}, {"text": "It tiles your windows.", "t": 14.84, "end": 16.226}, {"text": "They sit side by side, nothing on top of anything else, and they stay just where you put them.", "t": 16.544, "end": 21.63}, {"text": "It also gives you numbered workspaces.", "t": 22.053, "end": 24.08}, {"text": "If something's in the wrong place, you don't close it \u2014 you just move it to another number.", "t": 24.44, "end": 28.623}, {"text": "My work went on one, and my messages on two.", "t": 29.008, "end": 31.92}, {"text": "I had a camera on my front door, and that's only another window, so I put it on three.", "t": 32.318, "end": 36.72}, {"text": "You can float a window too, if you want to keep an eye on it.", "t": 37.109, "end": 39.52}, {"text": "I found it pleasing.", "t": 39.975, "end": 40.97}, {"text": "So I bought one or two more, and I gave each of my neighbours a workspace of their own.", "t": 44.495, "end": 48.553}, {"text": "Alan is four.", "t": 48.953, "end": 50.033}, {"text": "Juliana is five.", "t": 50.393, "end": 51.735}, {"text": "Dax is six.", "t": 52.095, "end": 53.24}, {"text": "I like to know my neighbours.", "t": 53.622, "end": 55.022}, {"text": "Dax's hedge was a little over the limit, so I took a few photographs, for the record.", "t": 57.882, "end": 62.671}, {"text": "And Juliana and I disagreed about which day the bins go out.", "t": 68.531, "end": 71.731}, {"text": "I'd kept seven empty, because it's useful to have somewhere to put things.", "t": 76.131, "end": 79.78}, {"text": "I moved Juliana there in March, and I've looked after her cat ever since.", "t": 80.192, "end": 83.753}, {"text": "I moved Alan in June.", "t": 86.118, "end": 87.393}, {"text": "Where is Dax now?", "t": 88.493, "end": 89.575, "who": "detective"}, {"text": "He's on seven, with the other two.", "t": 90.66, "end": 93.02}, {"text": "And what is on seven?", "t": 94.735, "end": 96.253, "who": "detective"}, {"text": "Only the one window.", "t": 97.078, "end": 98.255}, {"text": "They're side by side, nothing on top of anything else, and they stay just where I put them.", "t": 98.575, "end": 103.331}, {"text": "You should try it, Detective.", "t": 105.526, "end": 106.836}, {"text": "You look like a woman with a great many windows open.", "t": 107.264, "end": 109.504}], "voice": {"tape": {"stage": "tape", "text": "From the start? All right. A year ago I had forty windows open and couldn't find anything. Then I installed a small program called Spaceman. It tiles your windows. They sit side by side, nothing on top of anything else, and they stay just where you put them. It also gives you numbered workspaces. If something's in the wrong place, you don't close it you just move it to another number. My work went on one, and my messages on two. I had a camera on my front door, and that's only another window, so I put it on three. You can float a window too, if you want to keep an eye on it. I found it pleasing. So I bought one or two more, and I gave each of my neighbours a workspace of their own. Alan is four. Juliana is five. Dax is six. I like to know my neighbours. Dax's hedge was a little over the limit, so I took a few photographs, for the record. And Juliana and I disagreed about which day the bins go out. I'd kept seven empty, because it's useful to have somewhere to put things. I moved Juliana there in March, and I've looked after her cat ever since. I moved Alan in June. He's on seven, with the other two. Only the one window. They're side by side, nothing on top of anything else, and they stay just where I put them. You should try it, Detective. You look like a woman with a great many windows open.", "takes": [{"file": "voice-tape.mp3", "dur": 112.9